# A TESTBENCH LIVELOCK IN THE DEBUG-REQUEST DRIVER: the request could be held for a whole run, and the program
# could not advance while it was. Found triaging a round-1 dry-run failure of gen_ut_export_dbg_storm.
#
# THE DEFECT, with every gating term quoted from the sources this landing changes.
#   The autonomous storm request is issued under this guard:
#     gen_agents_pkg.sv   if (event_mean != 0 && !vif.req) begin
#                           if ($urandom_range(event_mean - 1, 0) == 0) cmd(1'b1, 1, 0);
#   so it is gated on THE LINE BEING LOW and NOT on the core being outside debug mode, and it asserts with hold
#   policy 1, UNTIL_DEBUG_MODE. That policy's release read:
#     gen_agents_pkg.sv   else if (hold_policy == 1 && bvif.evt_dbg_entered != entered_q) cmd(1'b0, 0, 0);
#   and the flag it watches is a TOGGLE flipped only on a debug-mode ENTRY EDGE:
#     gen_rvfi_pkg.sv     if (t.ext_debug_mode && !dbg_mode_q) bvif.evt_dbg_entered = ~bvif.evt_dbg_entered;
#   So a request asserted while the core was ALREADY in debug mode could never be released: no fresh entry edge can
#   occur, the toggle never flips, and the line stays asserted for the rest of the run. The DUT then correctly
#   remains in debug mode, and the program makes no further progress.
#
# WHY IT IS A HOLE AND NOT A TUNING PROBLEM: the driver acted on a state it could not see. The interface published
# only the entry toggle; there was no debug-mode LEVEL anywhere on the bridge, and the debug interface carries only
# the request line. A policy that means "hold until the core is in debug mode" was implemented against an edge.
#
# THE FIX: publish the level the scoreboard already tracks and test it.
#   gen_bridge_if.sv   a new evt_dbg_mode level beside the entry toggle
#   gen_rvfi_pkg.sv    set from the same place that maintains dbg_mode_q
#   gen_agents_pkg.sv  the UNTIL_DEBUG_MODE release now tests that level
# A request asserted while the core is already in debug has its condition satisfied on the spot and is released at
# the next negedge, so the case that used to hang now costs one cycle. The entry toggle is unchanged and its Python
# consumers keep working. The edge sampler the old release needed is removed rather than left dead.
# NO CONSTANT WAS ADDED. A cycle cap on the hold would have released the line without fixing the wrong-quantity bug
# and would have introduced a magic number; that alternative was rejected.
#
# THE ONE LIMIT, STATED: the level is derived from RETIRED records, so it lags the hardware by the retirement
# latency. That makes a release a few cycles late rather than never, which is the whole difference between a slow
# release and a hang. It is also why the assert is NOT additionally gated on the level: gating a lagging level would
# still admit the occasional assert-while-in-debug, and the release fix covers that case by construction.
#
# NOT A DUT FINDING, and the record stream says so rather than an argument: while the line was held the DUT stayed
# in debug mode, which is exactly what a held debug request means.

== 1. THE FAILURE, as the round-1 dry run produced it ==
   Entry gen_ut_export_dbg_storm, seed 700483392, head-mode at 962d31f.
$ command grep -a "AssertionError" LSF/sim_stdout.log | head -1
                                                           AssertionError: GEN_UT_EXPORT: no tohost store (SimTimeoutError)
   Zero UVM_ERROR and zero UVM_FATAL: the log's own severity counts read
   so no checker fired; the run died on the test's own wait for the program's end-of-test store.

