== 5. THE IRQ DRIVER WAS AUDITED FOR THE SAME HOLE AND DOES NOT HAVE IT ==
   Structural reason: its UNTIL_TAKEN release keys on evt_irq_taken toggling AND the entry's vector cause
   naming the line, a RECURRING per-entry event rather than entry into a persistent state, so a later entry
   always offers another release. Its other policies are self-limiting (a cycle countdown) or externally
   driven (the irq-ack handler). And decisively, a held interrupt line does not halt program progress, whereas
   debug mode does, so holding cannot starve the program the way the debug hole did.
   EMPIRICAL CONTROL, both round-1 irq-storm entries, read from their own logs:
     gen_ut_export_irq_storm: UVM_ERROR : 0  GEN_IRQ] events=833 releases=602 regime_events=231 final_levels=00000
     gen_ut_lockstep_div_timing_irq: UVM_ERROR : 0  GEN_IRQ] events=605 releases=300 regime_events=250 final_levels=00000
     Nothing left asserted at the end of either, and neither carries an error.
   TWO CAVEATS RECORDED, NEITHER A HANG: the irq regime engine is not gated on the line being low, so a fresh
   assert on an already-high line overwrites that line's hold policy and countdown; and UNTIL_ACK depends on
   the program reaching the ack handler, so a program without one holds that line to the end. Both leave the
   program running, which is why neither is fixed here.
# END. Every figure above is read from a run's own artefacts by the command printed above it. The export
# parsing takes the debug-mode flag and the cycle from the record's own fields.
